1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
LECTURE 1 — CONTAINER SCANNING WITH TRIVY

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,136
Your application is secure.

3
00:00:08,681 --> 00:00:12,317
Your database is encrypted.

4
00:00:12,861 --> 00:00:16,498
Your logs are immutable.

5
00:00:17,042 --> 00:00:29,769
And none of that matters if the container image you just pushed to production

6
00:00:30,313 --> 00:00:43,041
is quietly carrying a critical vulnerability from six months ago that nobody ever patched,

7
00:00:43,585 --> 00:00:55,403
sitting one layer down, in a base image you didn't even write yourself.

8
00:00:55,947 --> 00:01:08,675
Here's the mental model for this entire phase, and it's worth carrying all the

9
00:01:09,219 --> 00:01:14,674
way through every lecture that follows.

10
00:01:15,218 --> 00:01:23,400
You didn't write most of your own supply chain.

11
00:01:23,944 --> 00:01:36,671
Your base image was built by someone else, on a Linux distribution maintained by

12
00:01:37,215 --> 00:01:49,943
someone else, running a kernel written by thousands of people you'll never meet, pulling

13
00:01:50,487 --> 00:01:59,578
in Python packages authored by strangers across the entire internet.

14
00:02:00,122 --> 00:02:12,849
Every single one of those layers can carry a vulnerability, and every single one

15
00:02:13,394 --> 00:02:26,121
of those vulnerabilities is now, whether you like it or not, your production risk

16
00:02:26,665 --> 00:02:31,211
the moment you ship it.

17
00:02:31,755 --> 00:02:44,482
Supply chain security isn't really about your code at all, it's about everyone else's

18
00:02:45,026 --> 00:02:52,299
code that your code happens to depend on.

19
00:02:52,843 --> 00:03:05,571
Trivy is the tool that actually makes this manageable, and it's genuinely excellent at

20
00:03:06,115 --> 00:03:18,842
what it does, fast, accurate, open source, and it checks both your operating system

21
00:03:19,387 --> 00:03:28,477
packages and your language level dependencies in a single pass.

22
00:03:29,022 --> 00:03:41,749
Let's talk about configuration first, because this is exactly where teams either build a

23
00:03:42,293 --> 00:03:55,021
scanner that actually protects them or build one that trains everyone to ignore its

24
00:03:55,565 --> 00:03:59,201
output within a month.

25
00:03:59,745 --> 00:04:12,473
The first real decision is severity thresholds, and here's the senior level insight that

26
00:04:13,017 --> 00:04:16,653
most tutorials skip entirely.

27
00:04:17,198 --> 00:04:29,016
Failing your build on every single medium severity finding sounds thorough and responsible.

28
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:42,287
In practice, it trains your engineers to treat a failed pipeline as background noise,

29
00:04:42,832 --> 00:04:55,559
because medium severity findings are common, frequently unexploitable in your specific context, and blocking

30
00:04:56,103 --> 00:05:08,830
merges over them constantly teaches people to route around your security tooling rather than

31
00:05:09,375 --> 00:05:11,193
respect it.

32
00:05:11,737 --> 00:05:17,192
Fail hard on critical and high.

33
00:05:17,736 --> 00:05:30,463
Report medium for visibility and genuine tracking, but don't let it block a merge.

34
00:05:31,008 --> 00:05:43,735
A scanner everyone respects because it only screams when something genuinely matters is worth

35
00:05:44,279 --> 00:05:54,279
infinitely more than one that cries wolf on every single build.

36
00:05:54,823 --> 00:06:07,551
Let's wire this into GitHub Actions properly, running on every pull request and on

37
00:06:08,095 --> 00:06:20,822
a nightly schedule too, because here's a detail that catches teams off guard constantly.

38
00:06:21,366 --> 00:06:34,094
A dependency can be perfectly clean the day you merge it and develop a

39
00:06:34,638 --> 00:06:47,365
brand new disclosed vulnerability three weeks later, with zero code changes on your end

40
00:06:47,909 --> 00:06:48,819
whatsoever.

41
00:06:49,363 --> 00:07:00,272
Pull request scanning alone only catches problems that exist at merge time.

42
00:07:00,816 --> 00:07:13,543
A scheduled nightly scan is what catches the world changing underneath code you already

43
00:07:14,088 --> 00:07:18,633
shipped and haven't touched since.

44
00:07:19,177 --> 00:07:31,905
When Trivy finds something, it gives you a clean, specific table, the library, the

45
00:07:32,449 --> 00:07:45,176
CVE identifier, the severity, your currently installed version, and the fixed version, if one

46
00:07:45,721 --> 00:07:48,448
actually exists yet.

47
00:07:48,992 --> 00:08:01,719
That last column matters enormously, because it splits every finding into two completely different

48
00:08:02,264 --> 00:08:04,991
categories of problem.

49
00:08:05,535 --> 00:08:18,262
Vulnerabilities with a fixed version available are a to-do list item, bump the dependency,

50
00:08:18,807 --> 00:08:20,625
rebuild, done.

51
00:08:21,169 --> 00:08:33,896
Vulnerabilities with no fixed version available yet are a genuinely different problem, one you

52
00:08:34,441 --> 00:08:47,168
can't simply patch your way out of, and that distinction should directly shape how

53
00:08:47,712 --> 00:08:55,894
you triage and prioritize what you look at first.

54
00:08:56,438 --> 00:09:09,166
Here's where a lot of teams get the ignore file wrong, and it's worth

55
00:09:09,710 --> 00:09:22,437
being precise about it, because a sloppy trivyignore file is itself a real audit

56
00:09:22,981 --> 00:09:26,618
finding waiting to happen.

57
00:09:27,162 --> 00:09:39,889
Every single entry needs a reason, in a comment, right above the CVE number,

58
00:09:40,434 --> 00:09:47,706
not just the bare identifier sitting there unexplained.

59
00:09:48,251 --> 00:10:00,069
Is it a false positive, genuinely not exploitable in your specific runtime configuration.

60
00:10:00,613 --> 00:10:13,340
Is it a dev only dependency that never actually ships in your final production

61
00:10:13,885 --> 00:10:14,794
image.

62
00:10:15,338 --> 00:10:28,065
Is it something you're accepting temporarily while you wait on an upstream fix, with

63
00:10:28,609 --> 00:10:34,973
an actual expected date attached to it.

64
00:10:35,517 --> 00:10:48,245
An auditor who opens your ignore file and finds five bare CVE numbers with

65
00:10:48,789 --> 00:11:01,516
zero explanation attached to any of them is looking at exactly the kind of

66
00:11:02,060 --> 00:11:14,788
thing that turns into a real finding, because it looks indistinguishable from a team

67
00:11:15,332 --> 00:11:28,059
that got tired of red X's and just silenced them to make the pipeline

68
00:11:28,604 --> 00:11:29,513
pass.

69
00:11:30,057 --> 00:11:41,875
The mental model to carry forward into every other lecture in this phase.

70
00:11:42,419 --> 00:11:55,147
A scanner is only as valuable as the discipline around what happens after it

71
00:11:55,691 --> 00:11:57,509
finds something.

72
00:11:58,053 --> 00:12:08,962
Finding a vulnerability is the easy part, genuinely, it's almost entirely automated.

73
00:12:09,507 --> 00:12:22,234
Deciding what to do about it, fast, consistently, and defensibly, is the actual security

74
00:12:22,778 --> 00:12:35,506
program, and that's exactly what the rest of this phase is going to build,

75
00:12:36,050 --> 00:12:38,777
piece by piece.

76
00:12:39,321 --> 00:12:52,049
Let's make the layered dependency risk genuinely concrete, because it's easy to nod along

77
00:12:52,593 --> 00:13:05,320
with the concept in the abstract and much harder to actually feel it until

78
00:13:05,864 --> 00:13:14,046
you've traced one real example all the way down.

79
00:13:14,591 --> 00:13:26,409
Your production container starts from a base image, say a slim Python distribution.

80
00:13:26,953 --> 00:13:39,680
That base image itself was built from an even more minimal Linux distribution underneath

81
00:13:40,225 --> 00:13:41,134
it.

82
00:13:41,678 --> 00:13:54,405
That distribution ships OpenSSL, curl, a C standard library, dozens of small utility packages

83
00:13:54,949 --> 00:14:04,040
nobody on your team ever consciously chose or reviewed individually.

84
00:14:04,585 --> 00:14:17,312
Layer your own application on top, FastAPI, which itself depends on Starlette, which depends

85
00:14:17,856 --> 00:14:23,311
on AnyIO, which depends on Sniffio.

86
00:14:23,855 --> 00:14:36,582
By the time you've actually traced the full tree honestly, a genuinely simple looking

87
00:14:37,126 --> 00:14:49,854
application can easily depend, transitively, on several hundred distinct packages, and every single one

88
00:14:50,398 --> 00:15:03,125
of them is a door someone else built, that you're nonetheless fully responsible for

89
00:15:03,670 --> 00:15:05,488
keeping locked.

90
00:15:06,032 --> 00:15:18,759
Here's a mental model worth carrying specifically into how you read a Trivy report,

91
00:15:19,304 --> 00:15:32,031
because the raw list of findings, on its own, can feel genuinely overwhelming the

92
00:15:32,575 --> 00:15:39,848
first time you actually see it in full.

93
00:15:40,392 --> 00:15:48,574
Sort by exploitability, not merely by severity score alone.

94
00:15:49,118 --> 00:16:01,845
A critical vulnerability that requires local physical access to your machine to exploit is

95
00:16:02,390 --> 00:16:15,117
a genuinely different, much lower priority problem than a critical vulnerability that's remotely exploitable

96
00:16:15,661 --> 00:16:26,570
over an open network port your application actually exposes to the internet.

97
00:16:27,115 --> 00:16:39,842
Trivy's own output includes enough real context, the actual affected function, whether it's genuinely

98
00:16:40,386 --> 00:16:53,113
reachable given how you're actually using that specific package, to make this kind of

99
00:16:53,658 --> 00:17:06,385
informed, senior level triage decision, rather than simply treating every single critical finding as

100
00:17:06,929 --> 00:17:11,475
flatly, uniformly equal in urgency.

101
00:17:12,019 --> 00:17:19,292
One more genuinely practical habit worth building early.

102
00:17:19,836 --> 00:17:32,563
When you do add a brand new dependency to your project, spend thirty honest

103
00:17:33,108 --> 00:17:41,289
seconds actually checking it before you commit to it.

104
00:17:41,834 --> 00:17:47,288
How actively is it genuinely maintained.

105
00:17:47,832 --> 00:17:53,287
When was its last real release.

106
00:17:53,831 --> 00:18:03,831
How many other open issues does it currently have sitting unresolved.

107
00:18:04,375 --> 00:18:17,103
A package that hasn't been meaningfully touched by its maintainers in three years is

108
00:18:17,647 --> 00:18:30,374
a much higher long term risk than its current, momentarily clean scan result might

109
00:18:30,919 --> 00:18:43,646
otherwise suggest, precisely because nobody is actively watching it closely enough to catch and

110
00:18:44,190 --> 00:18:53,281
patch the next vulnerability whenever it eventually, inevitably gets disclosed.

111
00:18:53,825 --> 00:18:59,280
One last habit worth naming directly.

112
00:18:59,824 --> 00:19:12,551
Treat your severity thresholds as a living decision, not a one time setting you

113
00:19:13,096 --> 00:19:18,550
configure once and forget about forever.

114
00:19:19,094 --> 00:19:31,822
Revisit them every single quarter alongside your broader remediation review, and ask honestly whether

115
00:19:32,366 --> 00:19:45,093
critical and high are still catching the right things, or whether your own environment

116
00:19:45,638 --> 00:19:57,456
has shifted enough that the thresholds themselves now need a deliberate, documented adjustment.

117
00:19:59,456 --> 00:20:02,956
LECTURE 2 — DEPENDENCY SCANNING AND AUTOMATED UPDATES

118
00:20:03,956 --> 00:20:11,293
Container scanning catches vulnerabilities sitting in your operating system packages.

119
00:20:11,593 --> 00:20:21,866
But your actual application logic depends on a completely separate universe of risk, your

120
00:20:22,166 --> 00:20:32,439
Python dependencies specifically, FastAPI, Pydantic, SQLAlchemy, LangChain, every single package you imported to avoid

121
00:20:32,739 --> 00:20:36,407
writing something yourself from scratch.

122
00:20:36,707 --> 00:20:46,980
Each one of those is a door into your system that somebody else built,

123
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:57,553
and each one needs its own dedicated scanning, because a clean container base image

124
00:20:57,853 --> 00:21:08,125
tells you absolutely nothing about whether the actual application code layered on top of

125
00:21:08,425 --> 00:21:10,626
it is safe.

126
00:21:10,926 --> 00:21:18,998
Here's the mental model worth holding onto for this entire lecture.

127
00:21:19,298 --> 00:21:29,570
Every dependency you add to your project is a permanent, standing invitation for someone

128
00:21:29,870 --> 00:21:35,007
else's future mistake to become your incident.

129
00:21:35,307 --> 00:21:45,579
That's not a reason to avoid dependencies entirely, that would be absurd, nobody's rewriting

130
00:21:45,879 --> 00:21:52,483
an HTTP client from scratch in twenty twenty six.

131
00:21:52,783 --> 00:22:03,056
It's a reason to treat every single addition to your dependency tree with the

132
00:22:03,356 --> 00:22:13,628
same seriousness you'd treat adding a new engineer with production access, because in a

133
00:22:13,928 --> 00:22:19,799
very real sense, that's functionally what you're doing.

134
00:22:20,099 --> 00:22:30,371
Pip-audit, maintained by the Python Packaging Authority itself, scans your actual pyproject.toml or requirements

135
00:22:30,671 --> 00:22:40,944
file directly against the known vulnerability database and tells you precisely which of your

136
00:22:41,244 --> 00:22:45,646
declared dependencies have a disclosed issue.

137
00:22:45,946 --> 00:22:56,219
Wire it into CI the same way we wired in Trivy, fail hard on

138
00:22:56,519 --> 00:23:06,792
critical findings, and pair it with Safety for a second, genuinely independent data source,

139
00:23:07,092 --> 00:23:17,364
because vulnerability databases don't always agree with each other perfectly, and a finding that

140
00:23:17,664 --> 00:23:27,937
shows up in two independent sources carries more weight than one that only shows

141
00:23:28,237 --> 00:23:31,906
up in a single feed.

142
00:23:32,206 --> 00:23:42,478
Now here's the part that actually separates a functioning dependency management program from a

143
00:23:42,778 --> 00:23:53,051
beautifully configured one that nobody trusts within three months, Dependabot fatigue, and this is

144
00:23:53,351 --> 00:23:59,221
a genuinely underrated failure mode worth naming directly.

145
00:23:59,521 --> 00:24:09,793
Dependabot, left completely unconfigured, opens a fresh pull request for every single minor version

146
00:24:10,093 --> 00:24:20,366
bump across every single dependency in your entire tree, and within about two weeks

147
00:24:20,666 --> 00:24:30,939
your engineers have started reflexively merging these without reading them at all, because there

148
00:24:31,239 --> 00:24:39,310
are simply too many to review individually with any real attention.

149
00:24:39,610 --> 00:24:49,883
That habit, once it forms, is exactly how a genuinely dangerous major version bump

150
00:24:50,183 --> 00:25:00,455
slips through completely unreviewed, buried in a pile of routine, harmless ones everyone's already

151
00:25:00,755 --> 00:25:04,424
trained themselves to rubber stamp.

152
00:25:04,724 --> 00:25:14,997
The fix is grouping, and it's a small configuration change with an outsized impact

153
00:25:15,297 --> 00:25:17,498
on actual behavior.

154
00:25:17,798 --> 00:25:28,071
Group your minor and patch updates together into a single combined pull request that's

155
00:25:28,371 --> 00:25:33,507
genuinely safe to skim and merge quickly.

156
00:25:33,807 --> 00:25:44,080
Let major version bumps, and anything explicitly tagged as a security update specifically, stand

157
00:25:44,380 --> 00:25:54,652
completely alone, unmissable, in their own dedicated pull request that actually gets real, individual

158
00:25:54,952 --> 00:26:00,822
attention rather than getting lost in a batch.

159
00:26:01,122 --> 00:26:11,395
This single grouping decision is the difference between Dependabot being a genuine safety net

160
00:26:11,695 --> 00:26:21,967
and Dependabot being exactly the kind of noise your team has quietly learned to

161
00:26:22,267 --> 00:26:24,469
tune out entirely.

162
00:26:24,769 --> 00:26:35,041
Let's push this even earlier in the pipeline, to the pre-commit hook, because catching

163
00:26:35,341 --> 00:26:45,614
a vulnerable dependency the moment someone types it locally is dramatically cheaper than catching

164
00:26:45,914 --> 00:26:55,453
it in CI after a full pull request review cycle has already started.

165
00:26:55,753 --> 00:27:06,025
A pre-commit hook running pip-audit against any change to your dependency file gives immediate,

166
00:27:06,325 --> 00:27:16,598
local feedback, right there in the terminal, before a pull request even exists for

167
00:27:16,898 --> 00:27:27,171
anyone else to review, and it changes the entire economics of the mistake, from

168
00:27:27,471 --> 00:27:37,743
something that costs a review cycle and an awkward comment thread to something that

169
00:27:38,043 --> 00:27:48,316
costs one engineer about four seconds of their own time, privately, before anyone else

170
00:27:48,616 --> 00:27:50,817
ever sees it.

171
00:27:51,117 --> 00:27:56,987
Here's the senior level framing to carry forward.

172
00:27:57,287 --> 00:28:07,560
Dependency scanning done well isn't really about the scanning technology itself at all, Trivy,

173
00:28:07,860 --> 00:28:16,665
pip-audit, Safety, they're all genuinely solid tools and mostly interchangeable in capability.

174
00:28:16,965 --> 00:28:27,238
It's entirely about designing the surrounding workflow so that real signal reaches a human's

175
00:28:27,538 --> 00:28:37,076
actual attention, and pure noise gets filtered out automatically before it ever does.

176
00:28:37,376 --> 00:28:47,649
Get that balance right, and your team trusts every single alert that reaches them.

177
00:28:47,949 --> 00:28:58,222
Get it wrong, and eventually nobody reads any of them at all, which is

178
00:28:58,522 --> 00:29:08,061
functionally identical, from a risk perspective, to having no scanning in place whatsoever.

179
00:29:08,361 --> 00:29:18,633
Let's build out the fatigue problem with a picture worth genuinely holding onto, because

180
00:29:18,933 --> 00:29:29,206
it explains exactly why so many teams' Dependabot configurations quietly rot into pure background

181
00:29:29,506 --> 00:29:36,110
noise within just a few months of initial setup.

182
00:29:36,410 --> 00:29:46,682
Imagine a smoke detector so sensitive it goes off constantly, for burnt toast, for

183
00:29:46,982 --> 00:29:55,054
steam from a hot shower, for someone simply cooking dinner normally.

184
00:29:55,354 --> 00:30:05,626
Within about two weeks, everyone in that house has learned to reflexively silence it

185
00:30:05,926 --> 00:30:16,199
the instant it starts beeping, without a single moment's real thought, and that exact

186
00:30:16,499 --> 00:30:26,771
same learned reflex is precisely what happens the moment a real, genuine fire eventually

187
00:30:27,071 --> 00:30:29,273
does break out.

188
00:30:29,573 --> 00:30:39,845
An unconfigured, unfiltered Dependabot setup is that exact same smoke detector, applied directly to

189
00:30:40,145 --> 00:30:50,418
your own dependency tree, and grouping, done properly and deliberately, is what turns it

190
00:30:50,718 --> 00:31:00,991
back into a smoke detector people genuinely still trust and respond to when it

191
00:31:01,291 --> 00:31:03,492
actually, meaningfully matters.

192
00:31:03,792 --> 00:31:14,064
Here's a genuinely useful mental exercise worth running honestly against your own current setup

193
00:31:14,364 --> 00:31:16,566
right now, today.

194
00:31:16,866 --> 00:31:27,138
Open your actual pull request history and count, honestly, how many Dependabot pull requests

195
00:31:27,438 --> 00:31:37,711
got merged over the past month with a commit message that's effectively just approve,

196
00:31:38,011 --> 00:31:46,082
with zero further comment or genuine scrutiny attached to it whatsoever.

197
00:31:46,382 --> 00:31:56,655
If that number is meaningfully high, your grouping strategy almost certainly needs real, deliberate

198
00:31:56,955 --> 00:32:07,228
tightening, because every single one of those quick, reflexive merges was a moment where

199
00:32:07,528 --> 00:32:17,800
a genuinely dangerous change could have slipped straight through completely unnoticed, hiding comfortably in

200
00:32:18,100 --> 00:32:24,704
plain sight among a dozen other harmless, routine ones.

201
00:32:25,004 --> 00:32:35,277
Let's also talk honestly about a genuinely underrated practice here, pinning your direct dependencies

202
00:32:35,577 --> 00:32:45,849
to exact versions rather than loose, permissive version ranges, specifically in your production lockfile.

203
00:32:46,149 --> 00:32:56,422
A loose range feels more convenient day to day, sure, you get new features

204
00:32:56,722 --> 00:33:02,592
and routine fixes automatically without lifting a finger.

205
00:33:02,892 --> 00:33:13,165
But it also means your actual build isn't fully reproducible on demand, and a

206
00:33:13,465 --> 00:33:23,737
subtle, unannounced behavioral change three minor versions upstream can quietly surface in production with

207
00:33:24,037 --> 00:33:34,310
genuinely zero corresponding code change on your own end to point back to as

208
00:33:34,610 --> 00:33:36,811
the obvious cause.

209
00:33:37,111 --> 00:33:40,780
Pin exact versions in production.

210
00:33:41,080 --> 00:33:51,352
Let Dependabot, specifically, be your single, deliberate, reviewed mechanism for moving those pins forward

211
00:33:51,652 --> 00:34:01,925
intentionally, on your own schedule, rather than letting a loose range do it silently,

212
00:34:02,225 --> 00:34:06,628
invisibly, for you in the background.

213
00:34:06,928 --> 00:34:12,798
And one final thought worth carrying forward specifically.

214
00:34:13,098 --> 00:34:23,370
The healthiest dependency programs I've seen treat every single merged Dependabot pull request as

215
00:34:23,670 --> 00:34:33,943
a tiny, cheap experiment, watched briefly after merge, not blindly trusted purely because CI

216
00:34:34,243 --> 00:34:36,444
happened to pass.

217
00:34:36,744 --> 00:34:42,614
A green pipeline proves your tests still pass.

218
00:34:42,914 --> 00:34:53,187
It does not, on its own, prove the new version actually behaves identically in

219
00:34:53,487 --> 00:34:57,156
production under real, live traffic.

220
00:34:59,456 --> 00:35:02,956
LECTURE 3 — SECRET DETECTION: GITLEAKS AND TRUFFLEHOG

221
00:35:03,956 --> 00:35:13,038
Here's the mental model that makes this entire lecture feel urgent rather than routine.

222
00:35:13,329 --> 00:35:22,412
A vulnerability in a dependency is a risk, something that could potentially be exploited

223
00:35:22,703 --> 00:35:30,488
by someone who finds it and knows what to do with it.

224
00:35:30,779 --> 00:35:39,862
A secret committed to Git is not a risk, it is an incident, immediately,

225
00:35:40,153 --> 00:35:49,235
the very second it's pushed, whether or not anyone's actually noticed or used it

226
00:35:49,527 --> 00:35:50,427
yet.

227
00:35:50,718 --> 00:35:59,800
The moment an API key lands in your Git history, you have to treat

228
00:36:00,091 --> 00:36:09,174
it as compromised, full stop, because Git history is effectively permanent, forkable, cloneable, and

229
00:36:09,465 --> 00:36:17,899
frequently mirrored in places you have zero visibility into and zero control over.

230
00:36:18,190 --> 00:36:27,273
Let's talk about why rotation alone doesn't actually fix this the way people instinctively

231
00:36:27,564 --> 00:36:29,510
assume it does.

232
00:36:29,801 --> 00:36:38,884
If a secret gets committed and then quickly deleted in a follow up commit,

233
00:36:39,175 --> 00:36:48,257
it still exists, completely intact, in your Git history, retrievable by literally anyone with

234
00:36:48,548 --> 00:36:57,631
read access to the repository, forever, unless you go through the genuinely painful process

235
00:36:57,922 --> 00:37:07,005
of rewriting history entirely, which is disruptive, risky, and something you want to avoid

236
00:37:07,296 --> 00:37:12,486
ever needing to do in the first place.

237
00:37:12,777 --> 00:37:21,859
This is exactly why prevention has to happen before the commit, not detection after

238
00:37:22,151 --> 00:37:29,936
it, and that's precisely the shift Gitleaks and TruffleHog exist to enforce.

239
00:37:30,227 --> 00:37:39,309
Gitleaks runs as a pre-commit hook, scanning your actual staged changes against a set

240
00:37:39,600 --> 00:37:48,683
of regex patterns tuned specifically to what real secrets in your stack actually look

241
00:37:48,974 --> 00:37:49,874
like.

242
00:37:50,165 --> 00:37:59,248
Generic secret detection, out of the box, catches obvious, well known patterns like AWS

243
00:37:59,539 --> 00:38:02,134
access keys reasonably well.

244
00:38:02,425 --> 00:38:11,507
But here's the senior level move that most teams skip, writing custom rules for

245
00:38:11,799 --> 00:38:20,881
your own specific vendors, your OpenAI keys, your Groq keys, your Vault tokens, each

246
00:38:21,172 --> 00:38:27,011
with a pattern tuned to their exact known format.

247
00:38:27,302 --> 00:38:32,492
Generic detection catches the common, well documented cases.

248
00:38:32,783 --> 00:38:41,866
Custom rules catch the specific things that would actually hurt you personally if they

249
00:38:42,157 --> 00:38:50,591
leaked, tuned to your own real infrastructure rather than someone else's generic template.

250
00:38:50,882 --> 00:38:59,964
Let's talk about the allowlist, because it's exactly as important, in its own way,

251
00:39:00,256 --> 00:39:09,338
as the detection rules themselves, and it's just as easy to get dangerously wrong.

252
00:39:09,629 --> 00:39:18,712
Your test fixtures probably contain fake, intentionally invalid API keys used purely for testing

253
00:39:19,003 --> 00:39:28,085
purposes, and those absolutely should not trip a real alert every single time someone

254
00:39:28,377 --> 00:39:30,972
touches that test file.

255
00:39:31,263 --> 00:39:40,345
But here's the discipline that matters enormously, an allowlist entry needs to be scoped

256
00:39:40,636 --> 00:39:49,719
as narrowly as humanly possible, a specific file path, a specific commit hash, never

257
00:39:50,010 --> 00:39:59,093
a broad pattern match that could accidentally suppress a genuinely real secret that happens

258
00:39:59,384 --> 00:40:05,223
to superficially resemble your test fixtures in some way.

259
00:40:05,514 --> 00:40:14,596
A too-broad allowlist is, in a very real and structural sense, a self-inflicted blind

260
00:40:14,887 --> 00:40:23,970
spot, one your own team built deliberately and then, worse, quietly forgot was even

261
00:40:24,261 --> 00:40:25,161
there.

262
00:40:25,452 --> 00:40:31,940
TruffleHog adds a second, genuinely complementary layer worth understanding precisely.

263
00:40:32,231 --> 00:40:41,313
Where Gitleaks matches known patterns, TruffleHog goes a meaningful step further and actually verifies

264
00:40:41,604 --> 00:40:50,687
many secrets live, against the real provider's own API, confirming whether a detected key

265
00:40:50,978 --> 00:41:00,061
is genuinely still active right now, not merely pattern shaped and superficially plausible looking.

266
00:41:00,352 --> 00:41:09,434
This distinction matters enormously for triage under real time pressure, an unverified pattern match

267
00:41:09,725 --> 00:41:16,862
might be a false positive worth a quick, low urgency look.

268
00:41:17,153 --> 00:41:26,235
A verified, currently active secret is an active incident, right now, and it needs

269
00:41:26,526 --> 00:41:33,663
to jump straight to the very top of everyone's queue immediately.

270
00:41:33,954 --> 00:41:43,036
Now let's talk about the part almost every course on this specific topic skips

271
00:41:43,328 --> 00:41:52,410
entirely, and it's genuinely the most important part of the whole lecture, what actually

272
00:41:52,701 --> 00:41:57,891
happens the moment a real secret gets detected.

273
00:41:58,182 --> 00:42:07,265
Blocking the commit is necessary, but it is nowhere near sufficient on its own.

274
00:42:07,556 --> 00:42:16,639
The leaked secret still needs to be revoked, immediately, at the actual provider, OpenAI,

275
00:42:16,930 --> 00:42:26,012
AWS, Vault, wherever it lives, because the commit being blocked locally does absolutely nothing

276
00:42:26,303 --> 00:42:35,386
to protect you if that exact same secret was, say, already pasted into a

277
00:42:35,677 --> 00:42:44,760
Slack message, a support ticket, or a shared document somewhere else entirely, completely outside

278
00:42:45,051 --> 00:42:46,997
Git's own reach.

279
00:42:47,288 --> 00:42:56,371
A mature program automates this specific step directly, a detected, verified secret triggers immediate,

280
00:42:56,662 --> 00:43:05,744
automatic revocation at the source, not a ticket quietly sitting in someone's queue waiting

281
00:43:06,035 --> 00:43:13,821
for a human to eventually get around to it sometime this week.

282
00:43:14,112 --> 00:43:18,004
The mental model to carry forward.

283
00:43:18,295 --> 00:43:27,378
Every layer we're building in this lecture, pre-commit, CI, verification, automated revocation, exists specifically

284
00:43:27,669 --> 00:43:36,752
because any single layer alone will eventually fail, someone will bypass a hook under

285
00:43:37,043 --> 00:43:46,125
real deadline pressure, a scanner will miss a genuinely novel format it's never seen

286
00:43:46,416 --> 00:43:53,553
before, a human will paste something somewhere they truly shouldn't have.

287
00:43:53,844 --> 00:44:02,926
Defense in depth here isn't paranoia dressed up in security language, it's a direct,

288
00:44:03,217 --> 00:44:12,300
honest acknowledgment that prevention alone is never perfectly reliable, and your actual response speed,

289
00:44:12,591 --> 00:44:21,674
the moment something genuinely does slip through, matters every bit as much as your

290
00:44:21,965 --> 00:44:25,857
prevention did in the first place.

291
00:44:26,148 --> 00:44:35,231
Let's make the permanence of Git history genuinely, viscerally concrete, because it's the single

292
00:44:35,522 --> 00:44:44,605
detail that changes how seriously a junior engineer treats this specific topic once they've

293
00:44:44,896 --> 00:44:48,139
actually seen it demonstrated firsthand.

294
00:44:48,431 --> 00:44:57,513
Clone any public repository with a reasonably long history and run a search directly

295
00:44:57,804 --> 00:45:06,887
against every single commit in that entire history, not merely the current, latest state

296
00:45:07,178 --> 00:45:09,124
of the files.

297
00:45:09,415 --> 00:45:18,498
It's genuinely common to find secrets that were committed and then deleted, in a

298
00:45:18,789 --> 00:45:27,872
completely separate follow up commit, months or even years ago, still sitting there, perfectly

299
00:45:28,163 --> 00:45:37,245
intact and fully retrievable, in that earlier commit, forever, unless someone deliberately, carefully rewrote

300
00:45:37,536 --> 00:45:44,024
that project's entire history to actually remove them for good.

301
00:45:44,315 --> 00:45:52,749
Deleting a file in a new commit does not delete it from history.

302
00:45:53,040 --> 00:45:58,879
It only removes it from the current, latest snapshot.

303
00:45:59,170 --> 00:46:08,252
This exact distinction is precisely why prevention has to happen before that first commit

304
00:46:08,543 --> 00:46:14,382
ever lands, not as a cleanup effort attempted afterward.

305
00:46:14,673 --> 00:46:23,756
Here's a genuinely useful mental model worth carrying forward for thinking about entropy specifically,

306
00:46:24,047 --> 00:46:32,481
since it's the actual underlying mechanism most secret detection tools quietly lean on.

307
00:46:32,772 --> 00:46:41,855
Real, cryptographically random secrets look meaningfully different, statistically, from genuine, ordinary English text or

308
00:46:42,146 --> 00:46:44,741
from typical, everyday code.

309
00:46:45,032 --> 00:46:54,114
A tool measuring entropy is essentially asking, does this specific string look sufficiently random

310
00:46:54,405 --> 00:47:03,488
to plausibly be a real, generated secret, rather than simply a normal, ordinary word

311
00:47:03,779 --> 00:47:05,725
or variable name.

312
00:47:06,016 --> 00:47:15,099
This is exactly why entropy based detection can occasionally flag a long, oddly-shaped base64

313
00:47:15,390 --> 00:47:24,473
encoded test fixture as a false positive, and it's also precisely why pure entropy

314
00:47:24,764 --> 00:47:33,846
checking alone genuinely isn't sufficient on its own, pairing it deliberately with specific, known

315
00:47:34,137 --> 00:47:43,220
patterns for your own actual vendors catches real secrets that entropy checking alone might

316
00:47:43,511 --> 00:47:46,106
otherwise miss or misjudge.

317
00:47:46,397 --> 00:47:55,480
Let's talk about one more genuinely underrated practice worth adopting here, the security dot

318
00:47:55,771 --> 00:48:04,854
txt file, or more specifically, a clearly documented, well known internal process for exactly

319
00:48:05,145 --> 00:48:10,983
how to report and handle a suspected leaked secret.

320
00:48:11,275 --> 00:48:20,357
In the heat of an actual, live incident, the very worst possible moment to

321
00:48:20,648 --> 00:48:29,731
be figuring out, for the first time, who exactly has the authority to revoke

322
00:48:30,022 --> 00:48:39,104
a given API key, or precisely which specific runbook to follow for that particular

323
00:48:39,396 --> 00:48:45,883
provider, is right in the middle of the incident itself.

324
00:48:46,174 --> 00:48:55,257
Document this clearly and thoroughly, well in advance, calmly, and test it periodically, deliberately,

325
00:48:55,548 --> 00:49:04,630
the exact same way you'd test any other genuinely critical runbook across your entire

326
00:49:04,922 --> 00:49:05,822
system.

327
00:49:06,113 --> 00:49:10,005
One final point worth making plainly.

328
00:49:10,296 --> 00:49:19,379
The strength of your entire secret detection program is measured, in practice, not by

329
00:49:19,670 --> 00:49:28,753
how many secrets it successfully catches, but by how few genuinely ever need catching

330
00:49:29,044 --> 00:49:38,126
in the first place, because Vault and dynamic credentials, covered earlier in this course,

331
00:49:38,417 --> 00:49:44,256
already removed most static secrets from your codebase entirely.

332
00:49:44,547 --> 00:49:47,791
Detection is your safety net.

333
00:49:48,082 --> 00:49:57,165
Removing the need for static secrets altogether is the actual, deeper fix underneath it.

334
00:49:59,165 --> 00:50:02,665
LECTURE 4 — SBOM GENERATION

335
00:50:03,665 --> 00:50:13,280
Here's a question your auditor will ask you eventually, and it's a genuinely reasonable

336
00:50:13,571 --> 00:50:23,186
one to ask, even though it catches a surprising number of engineering teams completely

337
00:50:23,477 --> 00:50:28,971
flat footed the first time they hear it.

338
00:50:29,262 --> 00:50:38,190
What, precisely, is running inside your production container, right now, this exact moment.

339
00:50:38,481 --> 00:50:48,096
Not what you think is running, based on your own mental model of what

340
00:50:48,387 --> 00:50:49,760
you built.

341
00:50:50,051 --> 00:50:54,172
What is actually, verifiably, currently running.

342
00:50:54,462 --> 00:51:04,078
A Software Bill of Materials, an SBOM, is the honest, complete answer to that

343
00:51:04,368 --> 00:51:13,984
exact question, a full, structured inventory of every single package, every library, every dependency,

344
00:51:14,274 --> 00:51:19,769
and every transitive dependency sitting inside your image.

345
00:51:20,059 --> 00:51:29,675
Let's talk about the mental model here, because it genuinely reframes why this specific

346
00:51:29,965 --> 00:51:39,581
artifact matters so much more than it might initially seem to on the surface.

347
00:51:39,871 --> 00:51:49,487
Think of an SBOM as a nutrition label for your software, but a dramatically

348
00:51:49,777 --> 00:51:59,393
more complete one, because it doesn't just list the handful of major ingredients you'd

349
00:51:59,683 --> 00:52:09,299
expect, it lists literally everything, down to every single transitive dependency three, four, five

350
00:52:09,589 --> 00:52:19,205
layers deep, that you almost certainly didn't even know you were shipping in the

351
00:52:19,495 --> 00:52:20,869
first place.

352
00:52:21,159 --> 00:52:30,775
Most teams, genuinely, are surprised the very first time they actually generate one, because

353
00:52:31,065 --> 00:52:40,681
the true list of what's inside a production container is almost always dramatically larger

354
00:52:40,971 --> 00:52:50,587
and more complex than what any single engineer could confidently reconstruct purely from memory

355
00:52:50,877 --> 00:52:56,372
or from reading through their own requirements file.

356
00:52:56,662 --> 00:53:06,278
Syft, from Anchore, generates these automatically, in either SPDX format, an open, ISO standardized

357
00:53:06,568 --> 00:53:16,184
format increasingly required for government and defense contracts specifically, or CycloneDX format, which tends

358
00:53:16,474 --> 00:53:26,090
to be somewhat more common and comfortable in the general enterprise security tooling ecosystem.

359
00:53:26,380 --> 00:53:27,754
Generate both.

360
00:53:28,044 --> 00:53:37,660
They're cheap to produce, essentially free as a CI step, and different customers or

361
00:53:37,950 --> 00:53:47,566
different auditors will sometimes have a genuine, specific preference for one format over the

362
00:53:47,856 --> 00:53:48,756
other.

363
00:53:49,047 --> 00:53:58,662
Here's why this actually matters practically, in a way that goes well beyond simply

364
00:53:58,953 --> 00:54:02,387
satisfying a compliance checkbox somewhere.

365
00:54:02,677 --> 00:54:12,293
Picture a brand new critical vulnerability getting disclosed tomorrow morning in some deeply nested

366
00:54:12,583 --> 00:54:22,199
logging library, four layers down inside a completely different package you depend on, one

367
00:54:22,489 --> 00:54:32,105
you've genuinely never heard of and never directly interacted with in your own code.

368
00:54:32,395 --> 00:54:42,011
Without an SBOM already sitting there, ready to query, answering am I actually affected

369
00:54:42,301 --> 00:54:51,917
by this specific new disclosure means manually, painstakingly tracing your entire dependency tree by

370
00:54:52,207 --> 00:55:01,823
hand, under real time pressure, exactly when you least want to be doing careful,

371
00:55:02,113 --> 00:55:03,487
unhurried archaeology.

372
00:55:03,777 --> 00:55:13,393
With an SBOM already generated and indexed, it's a single, immediate query, seconds, not

373
00:55:13,683 --> 00:55:23,299
hours, and that speed difference is precisely what an incident response program is actually

374
00:55:23,589 --> 00:55:28,397
built to deliver when it matters most.

375
00:55:28,688 --> 00:55:38,303
Let's talk about attestation, because generating an SBOM and actually proving it corresponds to

376
00:55:38,594 --> 00:55:48,209
the specific image someone's running in production are two genuinely different problems, and conflating

377
00:55:48,499 --> 00:55:52,620
them is a real, common gap.

378
00:55:52,911 --> 00:56:02,526
Cosign lets you cryptographically attest your SBOM directly to the image itself, creating a

379
00:56:02,817 --> 00:56:11,746
verifiable, tamper evident link between this exact SBOM and this exact image digest.

380
00:56:12,036 --> 00:56:21,652
Without that attestation step, an SBOM is just a document sitting somewhere, disconnected, with

381
00:56:21,942 --> 00:56:30,184
no strong guarantee it actually still matches what's genuinely running right now.

382
00:56:30,474 --> 00:56:40,090
With it, you can verify, on demand, that the SBOM you're looking at genuinely,

383
00:56:40,380 --> 00:56:49,996
provably describes the exact image currently deployed, not some earlier, possibly outdated version, and

384
00:56:50,286 --> 00:56:56,468
not something a determined attacker could have quietly substituted.

385
00:56:56,758 --> 00:57:06,374
Here's a genuinely useful verification habit worth building into your regular operational rhythm, not

386
00:57:06,664 --> 00:57:15,593
just something you configure once during initial setup and then forget about entirely.

387
00:57:15,883 --> 00:57:25,499
Periodically generate a fresh SBOM directly from your actual running container, live, in production,

388
00:57:25,789 --> 00:57:35,405
and diff it explicitly against the SBOM you generated back at build time in

389
00:57:35,695 --> 00:57:36,595
CI.

390
00:57:36,886 --> 00:57:46,501
Any meaningful difference between those two is a real, legitimate signal worth investigating immediately,

391
00:57:46,792 --> 00:57:56,407
either genuine configuration drift that crept in somewhere along the way, or, in a

392
00:57:56,698 --> 00:58:06,313
worse and more concerning case, unauthorized modification of the image after it was originally

393
00:58:06,604 --> 00:58:08,664
built and shipped.

394
00:58:08,955 --> 00:58:18,570
The mental model to carry forward, into the CVE SLA lecture that follows this

395
00:58:18,861 --> 00:58:20,234
one directly.

396
00:58:20,525 --> 00:58:30,140
An SBOM alone doesn't actually fix anything or patch anything on its own, and

397
00:58:30,431 --> 00:58:33,865
it was never meant to.

398
00:58:34,155 --> 00:58:43,771
What it does is turn what's actually inside my system, a question that used

399
00:58:44,061 --> 00:58:53,677
to require hours of manual investigation under real pressure, into a question you can

400
00:58:53,967 --> 00:59:03,583
answer, precisely and completely, in seconds, any time anyone genuinely needs to ask it,

401
00:59:03,873 --> 00:59:13,489
which is exactly the foundation the rest of this phase builds directly on top

402
00:59:13,779 --> 00:59:14,679
of.

403
00:59:14,970 --> 00:59:24,585
Let's make the surprising scale of a real SBOM genuinely concrete with an honest,

404
00:59:24,876 --> 00:59:30,370
specific number worth sitting with for a moment.

405
00:59:30,661 --> 00:59:40,276
A reasonably typical Python web application, one that feels, subjectively, fairly lean and minimal

406
00:59:40,567 --> 00:59:50,182
to the engineers who actually wrote it, frequently ships somewhere between two and four

407
00:59:50,473 --> 01:00:00,088
hundred distinct packages once you honestly count every single transitive dependency all the way

408
01:00:00,379 --> 01:00:01,279
down.

409
01:00:01,569 --> 01:00:11,185
Most engineering teams, genuinely, have never once seen that full, complete list laid out

410
01:00:11,475 --> 01:00:21,091
explicitly in front of them, because day to day, they only ever interact directly

411
01:00:21,381 --> 01:00:30,997
with the roughly fifteen or twenty packages they personally, consciously chose and imported themselves.

412
01:00:31,287 --> 01:00:40,903
An SBOM is precisely what makes that entire, otherwise invisible iceberg fully, honestly visible

413
01:00:41,193 --> 01:00:44,627
for the very first time.

414
01:00:44,918 --> 01:00:54,533
Here's a genuinely useful mental model for explaining SBOMs specifically to a junior engineer

415
01:00:54,824 --> 01:00:58,945
who's never encountered the concept before.

416
01:00:59,235 --> 01:01:07,477
Compare it directly to a grocery store ingredients label on packaged food.

417
01:01:07,767 --> 01:01:17,383
You, as the actual shopper, mostly care about the handful of headline ingredients prominently

418
01:01:17,673 --> 01:01:21,794
listed right at the very top.

419
01:01:22,085 --> 01:01:31,700
A food safety regulator, doing their actual job properly, cares about every single listed

420
01:01:31,991 --> 01:01:41,606
ingredient, all the way down, including the obscure preservatives and additives buried near the

421
01:01:41,897 --> 01:01:45,331
very bottom in tiny print.

422
01:01:45,621 --> 01:01:55,237
Your own engineering team mostly, understandably cares about your direct, top level dependencies day

423
01:01:55,527 --> 01:01:56,901
to day.

424
01:01:57,191 --> 01:02:06,807
Your auditor, and just as importantly, your own future incident response process, genuinely needs

425
01:02:07,097 --> 01:02:16,713
the complete, unabridged list, every single layer, all the way down, with nothing quietly

426
01:02:17,003 --> 01:02:18,377
left out.

427
01:02:18,668 --> 01:02:28,283
Let's also talk honestly about license compliance, because it's a genuinely real, practical use

428
01:02:28,573 --> 01:02:38,189
of an SBOM well beyond pure security concerns alone, and it's one that surprises

429
01:02:38,479 --> 01:02:46,721
plenty of teams the first time it actually comes up in practice.

430
01:02:47,012 --> 01:02:56,627
Some open source licenses carry real, meaningful obligations, some copyleft licenses specifically require you

431
01:02:56,918 --> 01:03:06,533
to open source your own code if you link against them in certain particular

432
01:03:06,824 --> 01:03:07,724
ways.

433
01:03:08,014 --> 01:03:17,630
Without a complete, accurate SBOM sitting there ready to reference, you genuinely have no

434
01:03:17,920 --> 01:03:27,536
reliable, systematic way to catch a problematic license buried four layers deep in your

435
01:03:27,826 --> 01:03:37,442
own dependency tree before it quietly becomes, potentially, a very real legal problem for

436
01:03:37,732 --> 01:03:39,793
your company later.

437
01:03:40,083 --> 01:03:49,699
This is exactly why a growing number of enterprise procurement teams now explicitly ask

438
01:03:49,989 --> 01:03:59,605
for a current SBOM directly alongside your SOC 2 report itself, they're not merely

439
01:03:59,895 --> 01:04:09,511
checking for known vulnerabilities, they're also actively checking for exactly this kind of hidden,

440
01:04:09,801 --> 01:04:11,862
unexpected legal exposure.

441
01:04:12,152 --> 01:04:16,273
One last thing worth saying plainly.

442
01:04:16,563 --> 01:04:26,179
An SBOM you generate once and never look at again is barely better than

443
01:04:26,469 --> 01:04:29,217
no SBOM at all.

444
01:04:29,507 --> 01:04:39,123
Build the habit of actually querying it, regularly, the moment a new CVE makes

445
01:04:39,413 --> 01:04:49,029
headlines, and that habit alone is what turns this entire lecture from a checkbox

446
01:04:49,319 --> 01:04:56,874
into a genuinely useful, load-bearing part of your incident response process.

447
01:04:58,874 --> 01:05:02,374
LECTURE 5 — CVE SLAS

448
01:05:03,374 --> 01:05:12,470
Finding a vulnerability is the easy part of this entire phase, genuinely, it's almost

449
01:05:12,762 --> 01:05:17,959
completely automated at this point in the course.

450
01:05:18,251 --> 01:05:27,347
Fixing it within a timeframe you can actually stand behind, consistently, defensibly, and prove

451
01:05:27,638 --> 01:05:36,734
after the fact, is where most vulnerability management programs quietly fall apart, and it's

452
01:05:37,025 --> 01:05:44,822
exactly the gap a CVE Service Level Agreement exists specifically to close.

453
01:05:45,113 --> 01:05:51,611
Here's the mental model worth holding for this entire lecture.

454
01:05:51,902 --> 01:06:00,998
An SLA without genuine, working enforcement behind it isn't actually a policy, it's a

455
01:06:01,289 --> 01:06:10,386
wish, politely written down in a document nobody ever revisits after the day it

456
01:06:10,677 --> 01:06:12,626
was first created.

457
01:06:12,917 --> 01:06:22,013
The number itself, seven days for critical, fourteen for high, isn't remotely the hard

458
01:06:22,305 --> 01:06:31,401
part here, picking a defensible number takes about five minutes of honest discussion with

459
01:06:31,692 --> 01:06:32,992
your team.

460
01:06:33,283 --> 01:06:42,379
The actual hard part, the part that separates a real program from a paper

461
01:06:42,670 --> 01:06:51,766
one, is building the automation that catches the exact moment a real vulnerability quietly

462
01:06:52,058 --> 01:07:01,154
crosses that line, without requiring any single human to remember to go check manually.

463
01:07:01,445 --> 01:07:10,541
Let's talk about how to actually set these numbers sensibly, because arbitrary, made up

464
01:07:10,832 --> 01:07:19,929
timelines get challenged immediately, and rightly so, the first time an auditor or an

465
01:07:20,220 --> 01:07:26,717
engineering lead pushes back and asks you to justify them.

466
01:07:27,008 --> 01:07:36,105
Critical vulnerabilities, generally CVSS scored nine or above, with active, known, real world exploitation

467
01:07:36,396 --> 01:07:45,492
already happening in the wild, genuinely deserve the tightest possible window, we used seven

468
01:07:45,783 --> 01:07:54,880
days in our policy specifically, sometimes even less depending on your particular risk tolerance

469
01:07:55,171 --> 01:08:00,369
and how exposed the affected component actually is.

470
01:08:00,660 --> 01:08:09,756
High severity vulnerabilities get meaningfully more room, fourteen days is a reasonable, defensible industry

471
01:08:10,047 --> 01:08:10,947
default.

472
01:08:11,238 --> 01:08:20,335
Medium and low get correspondingly longer windows still, thirty and ninety days respectively, because

473
01:08:20,626 --> 01:08:29,722
forcing your engineering team to drop everything currently on their plate for every single

474
01:08:30,013 --> 01:08:39,109
medium finding is exactly how you burn out the very people you're depending on

475
01:08:39,401 --> 01:08:46,548
to actually keep your systems genuinely secure over the long haul.

476
01:08:46,839 --> 01:08:55,935
Here's the part that's genuinely, distinctly senior level, and it's the piece almost every

477
01:08:56,226 --> 01:09:02,074
basic tutorial on this topic skips over entirely, exceptions.

478
01:09:02,365 --> 01:09:11,461
A rigid SLA with absolutely zero, hard coded, no exceptions ever pathway inevitably breaks

479
01:09:11,752 --> 01:09:20,849
the very first time you hit a real vulnerability with no fixed version available

480
01:09:21,140 --> 01:09:30,236
anywhere yet, from any upstream vendor, no matter how hard your own team tries

481
01:09:30,527 --> 01:09:34,426
to resolve it on your end.

482
01:09:34,717 --> 01:09:43,813
A well designed program has a genuine, formal exception process, not a quiet, informal

483
01:09:44,104 --> 01:09:53,201
way for people to casually ignore inconvenient findings whenever they feel like it, but

484
01:09:53,492 --> 01:10:02,588
a real, structured process requiring a documented reason, a specific named approver with real

485
01:10:02,879 --> 01:10:11,975
accountability, an actual documented compensating control describing exactly how the residual risk is being

486
01:10:12,267 --> 01:10:21,363
managed in the meantime, and critically, an explicit expiration date, because an exception with

487
01:10:21,654 --> 01:10:30,750
no expiration date attached to it has a strong, well documented tendency to quietly

488
01:10:31,041 --> 01:10:40,138
become permanent, and a permanent, never revisited exception looks, to any experienced auditor, functionally

489
01:10:40,429 --> 01:10:48,226
identical to a gap that somebody simply decided, once, never to fix.

490
01:10:48,517 --> 01:10:57,613
Let's talk about enforcement mechanics, because this is exactly where the automation genuinely earns

491
01:10:57,904 --> 01:11:01,803
its keep and pays for itself.

492
01:11:02,094 --> 01:11:11,190
A scheduled job runs daily, checks every single currently open finding's actual age against

493
01:11:11,481 --> 01:11:20,578
its own severity specific deadline, and the moment anything crosses that specific line, it

494
01:11:20,869 --> 01:11:29,965
fires an alert immediately, PagerDuty for a critical breach that genuinely deserves someone being

495
01:11:30,256 --> 01:11:39,352
woken up, Slack for a high severity breach that needs prompt but not middle

496
01:11:39,644 --> 01:11:42,243
of the night attention.

497
01:11:42,534 --> 01:11:51,630
This is precisely the same discipline we built in an earlier phase for remediation

498
01:11:51,921 --> 01:12:01,017
tracking generally, applied here specifically and narrowly to CVEs, because a gap that's known

499
01:12:01,309 --> 01:12:10,405
about internally but silently aging past its own committed deadline is measurably worse, in

500
01:12:10,696 --> 01:12:19,792
an auditor's eyes and in genuine practical reality, than a gap that was never

501
01:12:20,083 --> 01:12:26,581
formally committed to a specific timeline in the first place.

502
01:12:26,872 --> 01:12:35,968
Here's why this matters so much for your actual audit conversation specifically, beyond simply

503
01:12:36,259 --> 01:12:40,158
being generally good, tidy engineering hygiene.

504
01:12:40,449 --> 01:12:49,545
CC7.1 and PI1.1 both, in their own distinct ways, care deeply about whether you're

505
01:12:49,836 --> 01:12:58,933
actually monitoring your own environment and responding appropriately to what that monitoring genuinely surfaces.

506
01:12:59,224 --> 01:13:08,320
A CVE SLA policy, backed by real, verifiable automated enforcement and an honestly tracked

507
01:13:08,611 --> 01:13:16,408
compliance rate over time, is direct, concrete, falsifiable evidence of precisely that.

508
01:13:16,699 --> 01:13:25,796
Not we care about security, stated generically and unfalsifiably as a value, but here

509
01:13:26,087 --> 01:13:35,183
is our specific, defensible commitment, and here is the actual data proving we consistently

510
01:13:35,474 --> 01:13:40,672
meet it, month after month, quarter after quarter.

511
01:13:40,963 --> 01:13:47,460
The mental model to carry forward into image signing next.

512
01:13:47,752 --> 01:13:56,848
A vulnerability program without a genuine, enforced SLA finds problems forever, indefinitely, without ever

513
01:13:57,139 --> 01:14:04,286
meaningfully proving they consistently get fixed within any bounded, predictable timeframe.

514
01:14:04,577 --> 01:14:13,674
Finding is necessary, but on its own, finding alone proves absolutely nothing at all

515
01:14:13,965 --> 01:14:23,061
about your actual security posture, without a real, honestly measured commitment attached firmly to

516
01:14:23,352 --> 01:14:25,951
what happens immediately afterward.

517
01:14:26,242 --> 01:14:35,339
Let's make the exception process genuinely concrete with a realistic, honest example worth walking

518
01:14:35,630 --> 01:14:37,579
through in full.

519
01:14:37,870 --> 01:14:46,966
Say a critical vulnerability gets disclosed in a specific logging library you depend on,

520
01:14:47,257 --> 01:14:56,354
and the maintainer has publicly, openly acknowledged it but genuinely hasn't shipped a real

521
01:14:56,645 --> 01:15:05,741
fix yet, and, being fully honest with yourself, probably won't for at least another

522
01:15:06,032 --> 01:15:10,580
several weeks given their own public roadmap.

523
01:15:10,872 --> 01:15:15,420
Your formal SLA technically says seven days.

524
01:15:15,711 --> 01:15:24,807
You are now, unavoidably, past it, through absolutely no fault of your own team's

525
01:15:25,098 --> 01:15:27,048
effort or diligence.

526
01:15:27,339 --> 01:15:36,435
This is exactly the moment a well designed, formal exception process genuinely earns its

527
01:15:36,726 --> 01:15:37,626
keep.

528
01:15:37,917 --> 01:15:47,014
You don't simply, quietly ignore the finding and hope nobody happens to notice it.

529
01:15:47,305 --> 01:15:56,401
You formally document, on the record, precisely why you can't fix it directly yet,

530
01:15:56,692 --> 01:16:05,788
exactly what compensating control you've put in place instead, perhaps a network policy that

531
01:16:06,080 --> 01:16:15,176
specifically blocks external access to the one particular vulnerable code path, and a real,

532
01:16:15,467 --> 01:16:22,614
concrete, specific date when you'll genuinely revisit the whole situation again.

533
01:16:22,905 --> 01:16:32,002
Here's a mental model worth carrying forward specifically about how SLA compliance rate should

534
01:16:32,293 --> 01:16:41,389
actually be read and interpreted over time, because a single snapshot number, taken in

535
01:16:41,680 --> 01:16:50,776
complete isolation, genuinely tells you meaningfully less than the actual trend does across several

536
01:16:51,068 --> 01:16:53,017
consecutive measurement periods.

537
01:16:53,308 --> 01:17:02,404
A compliance rate of ninety eight percent this month, following a rate of eighty

538
01:17:02,695 --> 01:17:11,792
five percent the month before that, tells a story of real, genuine, measurable improvement.

539
01:17:12,083 --> 01:17:21,179
That exact same ninety eight percent figure, following a previous rate of ninety nine

540
01:17:21,470 --> 01:17:30,567
and a half percent the month before, might actually represent a small, early warning

541
01:17:30,858 --> 01:17:39,304
sign of things quietly, gradually getting slightly worse over time, not meaningfully better.

542
01:17:39,595 --> 01:17:48,692
Track the trend explicitly, deliberately, not merely the single current number sitting there in

543
01:17:48,983 --> 01:17:51,582
isolation on its own.

544
01:17:51,873 --> 01:18:00,969
Let's talk about one more genuinely practical detail worth getting right from day one,

545
01:18:01,260 --> 01:18:10,357
how you actually calculate age for a given vulnerability in the first place, because

546
01:18:10,648 --> 01:18:18,445
this single technical decision meaningfully changes your reported compliance numbers either way.

547
01:18:18,736 --> 01:18:27,832
Do you measure age starting from the CVE's original public disclosure date, or starting

548
01:18:28,123 --> 01:18:37,219
from the specific moment your own scanner first, actually detected it in your particular

549
01:18:37,511 --> 01:18:38,411
environment.

550
01:18:38,702 --> 01:18:47,798
These two dates can genuinely differ by weeks or even months in some real

551
01:18:48,089 --> 01:18:57,185
cases, and being explicit, consistent, and transparent about exactly which one you're using is

552
01:18:57,477 --> 01:19:06,573
precisely the kind of small, easily overlooked detail an experienced auditor will specifically ask

553
01:19:06,864 --> 01:19:11,412
you to clarify and justify during fieldwork.

554
01:19:11,703 --> 01:19:15,602
One final point worth carrying forward.

555
01:19:15,893 --> 01:19:24,989
A CVE SLA that only your security team knows about protects almost nothing in

556
01:19:25,280 --> 01:19:26,180
practice.

557
01:19:26,471 --> 01:19:35,568
Publish it somewhere every engineer can see, tie it directly into your existing on-call

558
01:19:35,859 --> 01:19:44,955
and remediation tracking tools from earlier phases, and make hitting it, quietly and consistently,

559
01:19:45,246 --> 01:19:54,343
part of what good engineering simply looks like on your team, not a separate,

560
01:19:54,634 --> 01:19:56,583
bolted-on compliance obligation.

561
01:19:58,583 --> 01:20:02,083
LECTURE 6 — IMAGE SIGNING WITH COSIGN

562
01:20:03,083 --> 01:20:12,115
Here's a question worth sitting with for a moment, because it exposes a gap

563
01:20:12,401 --> 01:20:20,788
most teams have never actually closed, or in many cases even consciously considered.

564
01:20:21,074 --> 01:20:23,655
You scanned your image.

565
01:20:23,941 --> 01:20:25,877
Zero vulnerabilities found.

566
01:20:26,163 --> 01:20:27,063
Great.

567
01:20:27,350 --> 01:20:36,382
How do you actually know, with real cryptographic certainty rather than simple, comfortable assumption,

568
01:20:36,668 --> 01:20:45,700
that the image running in production right now is genuinely the exact same image

569
01:20:45,987 --> 01:20:55,018
you scanned, and not something quietly swapped, modified, or tampered with somewhere along the

570
01:20:55,305 --> 01:21:04,336
way, in your registry, in your deployment pipeline, or anywhere else in between the

571
01:21:04,623 --> 01:21:05,523
two.

572
01:21:05,810 --> 01:21:14,841
Here's the mental model for this entire lecture, and it's a genuinely useful one

573
01:21:15,128 --> 01:21:20,934
to carry forward well beyond just container images specifically.

574
01:21:21,221 --> 01:21:30,252
Scanning proves a specific artifact was safe, at one particular, specific moment in time.

575
01:21:30,539 --> 01:21:39,571
Signing proves that the exact thing you're running right now is genuinely, provably that

576
01:21:39,857 --> 01:21:48,889
exact same artifact, unmodified, with nothing at all quietly swapped out somewhere in between.

577
01:21:49,175 --> 01:21:58,207
Without signing, your scanning work is, in a real structural sense, disconnected from what's

578
01:21:58,494 --> 01:22:07,525
actually running in production, a clean scan report sitting confidently in one place, with

579
01:22:07,812 --> 01:22:16,844
no strong, enforced, cryptographic guarantee that what's genuinely deployed still matches it at all.

580
01:22:17,130 --> 01:22:26,162
Cosign, from the Sigstore project, solves this through public key cryptography, and the actual

581
01:22:26,448 --> 01:22:34,190
mechanism is worth genuinely understanding rather than treating as pure, unexamined magic.

582
01:22:34,476 --> 01:22:43,508
You generate a key pair, a private key that signs your image during your

583
01:22:43,795 --> 01:22:52,826
CI build, and a public key that anyone, your own team or a completely

584
01:22:53,113 --> 01:23:02,145
external verifier, can use afterward to confirm a given image was genuinely signed by

585
01:23:02,431 --> 01:23:11,463
that specific, known private key, without that public key ever being able to forge

586
01:23:11,750 --> 01:23:16,265
a brand new signature on its own.

587
01:23:16,552 --> 01:23:25,584
The private key needs real, serious protection, store it in Vault, never sitting loose

588
01:23:25,870 --> 01:23:34,902
in a GitHub secret if you can genuinely avoid it, because if that private

589
01:23:35,189 --> 01:23:44,220
key ever leaks, someone else can now sign malicious images that will pass your

590
01:23:44,507 --> 01:23:52,248
own verification checks and look completely, indistinguishably legitimate to your own systems.

591
01:23:52,535 --> 01:24:01,566
Let's talk about the actual signing step in your pipeline, because the sequencing here

592
01:24:01,853 --> 01:24:08,949
matters more than it might initially seem to at first glance.

593
01:24:09,236 --> 01:24:11,171
Build the image.

594
01:24:11,458 --> 01:24:20,490
Push it to your registry, tagged specifically with your Git commit SHA, never simply

595
01:24:20,776 --> 01:24:29,808
latest, because latest is a mutable, movable tag by definition, and a mutable tag

596
01:24:30,094 --> 01:24:39,126
pointing at something is precisely the opposite of what you're trying to cryptographically guarantee

597
01:24:39,413 --> 01:24:40,313
here.

598
01:24:40,599 --> 01:24:45,115
Sign that specific, immutable, SHA tagged image.

599
01:24:45,402 --> 01:24:54,433
Then, separately, verify the signature immediately, right there in the same pipeline, before you

600
01:24:54,720 --> 01:24:59,236
ever consider it ready to deploy anywhere.

601
01:24:59,522 --> 01:25:08,554
That verification step inside CI feels redundant at first glance, you only just signed

602
01:25:08,841 --> 01:25:17,872
it yourself, thirty seconds ago, why bother immediately checking your own freshly created signature.

603
01:25:18,159 --> 01:25:27,191
But it's exactly the same discipline as testing your own backup restore process, proving

604
01:25:27,477 --> 01:25:36,509
the mechanism genuinely, verifiably works end to end, rather than simply trusting, on faith,

605
01:25:36,796 --> 01:25:44,537
that it probably does because the individual steps all appeared to succeed.

606
01:25:44,824 --> 01:25:53,855
Here's where this becomes a genuinely enforced control, rather than merely a nice, symbolic

607
01:25:54,142 --> 01:26:01,883
step performed once in your CI pipeline and then quietly forgotten about.

608
01:26:02,170 --> 01:26:11,202
A ClusterImagePolicy inside Kubernetes itself can require valid, verified signatures before the cluster will

609
01:26:11,488 --> 01:26:20,520
admit any given image at all, meaning an unsigned image, or an image signed

610
01:26:20,806 --> 01:26:29,838
with the wrong, unrecognized key, simply cannot run in your cluster, full stop, no

611
01:26:30,125 --> 01:26:39,156
matter how it arrived there or through which specific path someone tried to deploy

612
01:26:39,443 --> 01:26:40,343
it.

613
01:26:40,629 --> 01:26:49,661
This moves signature verification from something your CI pipeline politely, cooperatively does for you

614
01:26:49,948 --> 01:26:58,979
to something your actual runtime environment structurally, unconditionally enforces, and that distinction matters enormously

615
01:26:59,266 --> 01:27:08,298
the moment you're forced to consider a genuinely adversarial scenario, one where an attacker

616
01:27:08,584 --> 01:27:17,616
already has some level of access to your registry or your deployment pipeline and

617
01:27:17,903 --> 01:27:23,709
is actively trying to sneak something malicious past you.

618
01:27:23,995 --> 01:27:33,027
Let's also talk about keyless signing briefly, because it's worth knowing this alternative exists,

619
01:27:33,313 --> 01:27:42,345
even if a static key pair is often the simpler, more comfortable starting point

620
01:27:42,632 --> 01:27:47,148
for a smaller team just getting started.

621
01:27:47,434 --> 01:27:56,466
Sigstore's Fulcio service can issue extremely short lived certificates tied directly to your CI

622
01:27:56,752 --> 01:28:05,784
identity itself, your specific GitHub Actions workflow, for instance, meaning you never have to

623
01:28:06,071 --> 01:28:15,102
manage a long lived private key at all, anywhere, because the entire process is

624
01:28:15,389 --> 01:28:24,421
instead tied cryptographically to who you already, provably are within your existing CI system's

625
01:28:24,707 --> 01:28:25,997
own identity.

626
01:28:26,284 --> 01:28:35,316
It's a genuinely more modern approach, and it's worth adopting once your team has

627
01:28:35,602 --> 01:28:43,344
real, practical comfort with the basic mechanics of static key signing first.

628
01:28:43,630 --> 01:28:51,372
The mental model to carry forward, directly into policy as code next.

629
01:28:51,658 --> 01:29:00,690
Scanning without signing tells you what was true once, at one specific past moment.

630
01:29:00,977 --> 01:29:10,008
Signing, genuinely enforced at your actual runtime layer, tells you what's true right now,

631
01:29:10,295 --> 01:29:19,327
continuously, for every single thing genuinely running in your production environment, and that shift,

632
01:29:19,613 --> 01:29:28,645
from a one time, static point in time claim to an ongoing, continuously enforced,

633
01:29:28,931 --> 01:29:37,963
structural guarantee, is precisely what turns a security practice into a genuine, real security

634
01:29:38,250 --> 01:29:39,150
control.

635
01:29:39,436 --> 01:29:48,468
Let's make the actual risk of unsigned images genuinely concrete with a realistic scenario

636
01:29:48,755 --> 01:29:51,335
worth walking through carefully.

637
01:29:51,622 --> 01:30:00,653
Imagine your container registry credentials, the ones your CI pipeline itself uses to push

638
01:30:00,940 --> 01:30:09,972
new images, get compromised somehow, through a leaked token, a misconfigured permission, any real,

639
01:30:10,258 --> 01:30:12,839
plausible path at all.

640
01:30:13,125 --> 01:30:22,157
Without any image signing in place whatsoever, an attacker holding those exact credentials can

641
01:30:22,444 --> 01:30:31,475
push a modified, subtly backdoored image, tagged to look completely identical to your legitimate

642
01:30:31,762 --> 01:30:40,793
one, and your own Kubernetes cluster will accept and run it without a single

643
01:30:41,080 --> 01:30:50,112
moment's hesitation, because nothing at all in your entire deployment pipeline was ever actually

644
01:30:50,398 --> 01:30:59,430
checking whether that specific image genuinely, provably came from your real, legitimate CI pipeline

645
01:30:59,717 --> 01:31:02,297
in the first place.

646
01:31:02,584 --> 01:31:11,615
With enforced signature verification actively in place, that exact same attacker can still push

647
01:31:11,902 --> 01:31:20,934
their modified image successfully, sure, but your cluster will flatly refuse to run it,

648
01:31:21,220 --> 01:31:30,252
because they don't have your private signing key, and without that specific key, they

649
01:31:30,538 --> 01:31:38,280
simply cannot produce a signature your cluster will actually trust and accept.

650
01:31:38,566 --> 01:31:47,598
Here's a genuinely useful mental model for explaining the actual public key and private

651
01:31:47,885 --> 01:31:56,916
key relationship specifically to a junior engineer encountering asymmetric cryptography for the very first

652
01:31:57,203 --> 01:32:01,074
time in a hands-on, practical context.

653
01:32:01,360 --> 01:32:10,392
A private key is like a genuinely unique, physical signature only you personally can

654
01:32:10,679 --> 01:32:18,420
produce, one that's fundamentally, mathematically difficult for anyone else to convincingly forge.

655
01:32:18,707 --> 01:32:27,738
A public key is like a large, widely trusted reference book of known, verified

656
01:32:28,025 --> 01:32:35,766
signatures that anyone, anywhere, can freely check a document's actual signature against.

657
01:32:36,053 --> 01:32:45,085
You never, under any circumstances, hand out your own actual signing hand to anyone

658
01:32:45,371 --> 01:32:46,271
else.

659
01:32:46,558 --> 01:32:55,589
You do, quite freely and openly, hand out copies of that reference book to

660
01:32:55,876 --> 01:33:04,263
literally anyone who might ever need to verify something you've genuinely signed yourself.

661
01:33:04,549 --> 01:33:13,581
Let's talk about a genuinely underrated operational practice worth building into your regular routine

662
01:33:13,867 --> 01:33:22,899
here, key rotation for your signing key itself, distinct and separate from key rotation

663
01:33:23,186 --> 01:33:28,992
for your actual application credentials elsewhere in your stack.

664
01:33:29,278 --> 01:33:38,310
If your signing private key is ever compromised in any way, every single image

665
01:33:38,597 --> 01:33:47,628
you've ever signed with it becomes immediately, retroactively suspect and untrustworthy, not merely images

666
01:33:47,915 --> 01:33:52,431
signed going forward from that point on.

667
01:33:52,717 --> 01:34:01,749
This is exactly why a genuinely mature signing setup supports multiple valid, currently trusted

668
01:34:02,036 --> 01:34:11,067
signing keys simultaneously, one actively used for new signatures going forward, and older ones

669
01:34:11,354 --> 01:34:20,386
still explicitly, deliberately trusted for verifying genuinely legitimate historical images that were signed before

670
01:34:20,672 --> 01:34:24,543
the most recent rotation actually happened.

671
01:34:24,829 --> 01:34:28,700
One last thought worth holding onto.

672
01:34:28,987 --> 01:34:38,018
Signing only matters if verification is actually enforced somewhere real, not merely available as

673
01:34:38,305 --> 01:34:41,531
an option nobody's turned on.

674
01:34:41,817 --> 01:34:50,849
Audit your own cluster today, honestly, and confirm the enforcement policy is genuinely active,

675
01:34:51,135 --> 01:34:56,296
not simply sitting there configured and quietly ignored.

676
01:34:58,296 --> 01:35:01,796
LECTURE 7 — POLICY AS CODE WITH OPA CONFTEST

677
01:35:02,796 --> 01:35:04,466
You've scanned the image.

678
01:35:04,752 --> 01:35:06,004
