703
01:37:02,927 --> 01:37:08,770
You're writing a genuine, declarative statement of exactly what should never, under any circumstances,

704
01:37:09,056 --> 01:37:09,956
be true.

705
01:37:10,243 --> 01:37:13,999
A container running as root should never be true.

706
01:37:14,286 --> 01:37:18,042
A deployment missing resource limits should never be true.

707
01:37:18,328 --> 01:37:22,919
A service exposing a raw NodePort directly should never be true.

708
01:37:23,205 --> 01:37:29,048
Each individual rule is a single, specific, falsifiable claim, and Conftest simply, mechanically checks

709
01:37:29,335 --> 01:37:35,177
every one of those specific claims against your actual manifest, every single time, with

710
01:37:35,464 --> 01:37:39,637
zero fatigue and zero drift in how carefully it's applied.

711
01:37:39,924 --> 01:37:45,767
Let's talk about why this matters so specifically for SOC 2 evidence, beyond simply

712
01:37:46,053 --> 01:37:49,392
being generally good engineering hygiene worth having regardless.

713
01:37:49,678 --> 01:37:55,521
Manual code review of infrastructure manifests genuinely cannot produce reliable, consistent, auditable evidence, because

714
01:37:55,808 --> 01:38:01,650
it depends entirely, fundamentally, on an individual human's judgment and attention on that one

715
01:38:01,937 --> 01:38:07,780
particular day, which is precisely the kind of inconsistency an auditor is specifically trained

716
01:38:08,066 --> 01:38:10,153
to probe for and find.

717
01:38:10,439 --> 01:38:16,282
Automated policy enforcement produces a clean, mechanical pass or fail result for literally every

718
01:38:16,568 --> 01:38:22,411
single deployment, forever, and that consistency is exactly what turns we review our manifests

719
01:38:22,698 --> 01:38:28,540
carefully into concrete, verifiable proof, we mechanically, structurally enforce these specific, named standards on

720
01:38:28,827 --> 01:38:30,914
every single deployment without exception.

721
01:38:31,200 --> 01:38:37,043
Here's a genuinely practical discipline worth adopting from day one with any new policy

722
01:38:37,329 --> 01:38:43,172
you write, and it's a lesson every team eventually learns, usually the hard way,

723
01:38:43,459 --> 01:38:44,711
after an incident.

724
01:38:44,997 --> 01:38:50,840
Start every new policy in warn mode, not deny mode, for at least the

725
01:38:51,127 --> 01:38:54,883
first week or two of its life in production.

726
01:38:55,169 --> 01:39:01,012
Watch what it actually flags across your real, existing manifests before you let it

727
01:39:01,298 --> 01:39:02,968
start blocking merges outright.

728
01:39:03,254 --> 01:39:09,097
A policy that looks perfectly correct and reasonable on paper can accidentally, unexpectedly break

729
01:39:09,383 --> 01:39:15,226
a completely legitimate, existing deployment pattern you'd genuinely forgotten still existed somewhere in your

730
01:39:15,513 --> 01:39:21,355
infrastructure, and discovering that specific problem in warn mode, calmly, costs you absolutely nothing

731
01:39:21,642 --> 01:39:22,542
at all.

732
01:39:22,828 --> 01:39:28,671
Discovering that same exact problem in deny mode, for the first time, at two

733
01:39:28,958 --> 01:39:34,800
in the morning during an urgent, unplanned emergency deployment, costs you considerably, meaningfully more.

734
01:39:35,087 --> 01:39:40,930
Let's also talk honestly about policy sprawl, because it's a genuine, real failure mode

735
01:39:41,216 --> 01:39:44,555
worth naming directly and getting ahead of early.

736
01:39:44,841 --> 01:39:50,684
As your policy library naturally grows over time, ten rules, then twenty five, then

737
01:39:50,971 --> 01:39:56,813
eighty, keeping every single one of them genuinely relevant, accurate, and up to date

738
01:39:57,100 --> 01:40:02,943
with your evolving architecture becomes real, ongoing, ordinary maintenance work, not a one time

739
01:40:03,229 --> 01:40:07,403
setup task you complete once and then never revisit again.

740
01:40:07,689 --> 01:40:13,532
Review your policy set periodically, deliberately, the exact same disciplined way we've reviewed access

741
01:40:13,818 --> 01:40:19,661
grants, IAM roles, and vendor contracts throughout every other phase of this entire course,

742
01:40:19,948 --> 01:40:25,790
because a stale, outdated policy that no longer genuinely matches your current architecture is

743
01:40:26,077 --> 01:40:31,920
either annoyingly, needlessly blocking legitimate, correct deployments for no real reason, or, in the

744
01:40:32,206 --> 01:40:38,049
more dangerous case, has quietly, silently stopped catching the exact real problem it was

745
01:40:38,335 --> 01:40:42,091
originally, deliberately written to catch in the first place.

746
01:40:42,378 --> 01:40:46,551
The mental model to carry forward into our final lecture.

747
01:40:46,838 --> 01:40:52,681
Every single control in this entire phase, scanning, signing, policy enforcement, exists specifically to

748
01:40:52,967 --> 01:40:58,810
convert we believe our deployments are secure, a comfortable but ultimately unverifiable belief, into

749
01:40:59,096 --> 01:41:04,939
we can mechanically, consistently prove our deployments meet these exact, specific, named standards, every

750
01:41:05,226 --> 01:41:08,982
single time, for every single deployment, without exception, forever.

751
01:41:09,268 --> 01:41:15,111
Let's make Rego's declarative nature genuinely concrete with a comparison worth holding onto carefully,

752
01:41:15,397 --> 01:41:21,240
because it's exactly the specific detail that trips up engineers coming from a purely

753
01:41:21,527 --> 01:41:27,369
imperative programming background the very first time they actually sit down and try to

754
01:41:27,656 --> 01:41:29,743
write a real policy themselves.

755
01:41:30,029 --> 01:41:35,872
In a typical imperative language, you'd naturally write something like, check whether the container

756
01:41:36,158 --> 01:41:42,001
is running as root, and if it genuinely is, then explicitly raise an error

757
01:41:42,288 --> 01:41:43,188
yourself.

758
01:41:43,474 --> 01:41:49,317
In Rego specifically, you instead write a rule that simply, declaratively describes exactly what

759
01:41:49,603 --> 01:41:55,446
a genuine violation actually looks like, and the underlying engine itself handles checking that

760
01:41:55,733 --> 01:42:01,158
specific condition against every single input you ever give it, consistently, mechanically, forever.

761
01:42:01,445 --> 01:42:07,287
This shift, from telling the computer precisely how to check something step by step,

762
01:42:07,574 --> 01:42:13,417
to simply, clearly describing what a violation actually is in the first place, is

763
01:42:13,703 --> 01:42:19,546
genuinely the entire conceptual hurdle worth carefully explaining upfront to any junior engineer encountering

764
01:42:19,832 --> 01:42:23,171
policy as code for the very first time.

